<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- ================================================================= -->
<!--  Chapter 9: Understanding Variability                              -->
<!-- ================================================================= -->
<chapter xml:id="ch-variability" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Understanding Variability</title>

    <introduction xml:id="intro-ch10-variability">
        <p>
            In <xref ref="ch-variability"/>, we measured variability — how
            spread out values are around a centre, and how to put a number on
            that spread.
            We saw that life expectancy across African countries had a standard
            deviation of around 9.6 years in 2007, that Rwanda's collapse in
            1992 produced a squared distance from the mean so large it dominated
            the variance for that year, and that the IQR is more robust to such
            extremes than the standard deviation.
            Those are all answers to the question: <em>how much</em> does this
            vary?
        </p>

        <p>
            This chapter asks a different question: <em>why</em> does it vary?
            Or more precisely — what portion of the variation can we account
            for, and what portion remains unexplained?
            This is the distinction between the three types of variability
            introduced in <xref ref="sec-accounting-journeys"/>: accounted-for,
            unaccounted-for, and natural.
            Measuring tells you the size of the spread.
            Accounting tells you where it comes from.
        </p>
    </introduction>

    <section xml:id="sec-accounting-journeys">
        <title>The Same Journey, Every Day Different</title>

        <introduction>
            <p>
                In August 2026, on a Friday evening, I was leaving Nairobi's CBD
                to catch a late flight home to London.
                I had a flight at 11:40pm and ordered an Uber around 7:30pm.
                The first driver appeared, then cancelled while I was outside
                looking for them — I had lost wifi in the chaos of the streets
                and could not track them through the app.
                The same thing happened with a second driver.
                When I finally got wifi, the second driver agreed to pick me up
                from a hotel across the road, but had to turn around first
                because of the one-way street.
                I waited twenty minutes on that pavement, surrounded by cars,
                and could not find them anywhere.
            </p>

            <p>
                I eventually walked through the traffic hoping to find wifi and
                order a new driver somewhere away from the worst of the
                gridlock.
                A worker outside a café noticed me standing on the pavement,
                clearly lost and increasingly frantic, and insisted I come in.
                She sat me down, connected me to the wifi, and gave me a moment
                to think.
                Without that kindness I am not sure what I would have done.
                I caught the flight.
            </p>

            <p>
                The app said the third taxi was ten minutes away.
                It took forty-five.
                The app had estimated ten minutes — and I had believed it,
                because I had no reason not to.
                I sat watching the little car barely move, block after block, as
                the minutes ticked by and my flight window got smaller.
            </p>

            <p>
                Ask someone from Nairobi what happened, and they will tell you
                immediately: it was Friday evening, in the CBD, at 7:30pm.
                That is a journey you plan hours around, or you do not make it.
                The traffic leaving the central business district on a Friday
                evening is not unpredictable.
                It is entirely predictable.
                It is just that I did not know to predict it.
            </p>

            <p>
                In the previous chapter, we measured variability — how spread
                out values are, and how to summarise that spread in a single
                number.
                But measuring variability and understanding it are two different
                things.
                My taxi journey was not just variable.
                It was variable for reasons — some of which I should have known,
                some of which I could not have known, and some of which were
                simply random.
                This chapter is about separating those three things.
            </p>

            <p>
                Imagine you had no information at all about how long a boda-boda
                ride across Nairobi would take.
                No knowledge of rush hour, no sense of which roads are usually
                busy, no awareness that Fridays are worse than Tuesdays.
                Every day, you would treat each journey as if it were identical
                to every other — and you would be wrong, repeatedly, in ways
                that cost you time, money, and patience.
                You would book the same amount of time for a 7am ride and a 5pm
                ride.
                You would be surprised, every single Friday afternoon, by the
                traffic.
                You would have no way to plan.
            </p>

            <p>
                Now imagine you know one thing: rush hour is slower.
                Suddenly you can plan.
                You leave earlier on the days that matter, and book less time
                for the quiet morning run.
                You may still be wrong sometimes — you cannot know about the
                accident on Ngong Road, or the blocked junction in town — but
                you are systematically less wrong than before.
                Knowing even one source of variation and accounting for it makes
                your predictions better.
                My mistake on that Friday evening was not unavoidable.
                It was a failure to account for something that was entirely
                knowable.
            </p>

            <p>
                This is what statisticians mean by the three types of
                variability.
                Some variation is <term>accounted-for variability</term>: you
                know what causes it, you have measured it, and you can use it to
                make better predictions.
                Time of day is exactly this — once you know it is 5pm on a
                Friday in Nairobi's CBD, you already know something useful about
                the journey ahead.
                Some variation is <term>unaccounted-for variability</term>:
                other things affect the journey — accidents, roadworks, the
                school term starting — but you have not measured them and cannot
                include them in your predictions.
                They are real causes of variation, making your predictions less
                reliable even when you have accounted for everything else you
                can.
                And some variation is just <term>natural variability</term>: the
                boda-boda rider caught a green light, or took a slightly
                different line through the roundabout.
                No amount of information would have predicted it.
                It simply happened.
            </p>

            <p>
                The problem is not the natural variability — any honest
                prediction has to live with it.
                The problem is mistaking unaccounted-for variation for natural
                variation.
                If you assume that everything you cannot account for is just
                noise, you will underestimate how wrong your predictions can be,
                and you will miss the fact that some of that wrongness has a
                cause that could, in principle, be found and understood.
                A boda-boda rider who thinks the variation in their journey
                times is just random bad luck will never think to check whether
                there is a pattern — whether certain roads are systematically
                worse, whether the school term changes things, whether the
                weather is a factor.
                Those patterns are real.
                Mistaking them for noise means never looking for them.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-accounting-traffic">
            <title>Accounting for Traffic Variation in Nairobi</title>

            <p>
                The visualisations by TomTom displayed in Ch9 make the three
                types of variability visible in a way that numbers alone cannot.
            </p>

            <p>
                Recall <xref ref="fig-tomtom-heatmap-week"/> which shows travel
                time across every hour of every day of the week, averaged across
                2025.
                Each cell represents one two-hour slot on one day of the week;
                darker red means longer journey times, lighter pink means
                shorter.
                The pattern is immediately visible: weekday mornings and
                evenings are dark, weekend days are lighter, and the middle of
                the night is the lightest of all.
                This is <term>accounted-for variability</term> made visible: the
                variation that follows a predictable pattern, week after week,
                and that anyone who knows Nairobi already carries in their head.
                If I had looked at this chart before ordering my Uber on that
                Friday evening in August, I would have seen immediately that
                7:30pm on a Friday is one of the darker cells on the whole grid.
            </p>

            <p>
                <xref ref="fig-tomtom-map-morning"/> and
                <xref ref="fig-tomtom-map-evening"/> show where in Nairobi that
                congestion falls during the morning and evening rush hours — not
                just when, but where.
                Yellow hexagons indicate moderate congestion; orange and red
                indicate heavier delays.
                The CBD and the main arterial roads radiating outward from it
                are visibly darker than the outer suburbs.
                During the morning rush, the congestion concentrates in the city
                centre as commuters flow in; during the evening rush, it spreads
                more broadly as people try to get home.
                This spatial pattern is also displaying
                <term>accounted-for variability</term> — predictable,
                measurable, and useful for planning.
            </p>

            <p>
                But even knowing the time of day and the location is not enough
                to predict any specific journey perfectly.
                The fourth image illustrates why.
                This is a screenshot I have just taken showing live congestion
                in Nairobi right now <fn>25th August 2026, 18:48 EAT</fn>,
                against the usual congestion level for the same time slot.
                The orange line is what is happening right now, the dotted
                yellow line is what typically happens at this time.
                On Monday morning, live congestion spiked to nearly 90 percent,
                well above the usual level of around 65 percent at that hour.
                On Tuesday afternoon, at the time this image was captured, live
                congestion was running at over 100 percent, higher than the
                typical level, and still climbing.
                Something was making that particular day worse than a typical
                Tuesday.
                Perhaps an accident, or roadworks, or nothing identifiable at
                all.
            </p>

            <p>
                This is <term>unaccounted-for variability</term> in action.
                TomTom's usual congestion line represents what the accounted-for
                variability predicts, the pattern that repeats week after week.
                The gap between that line and the live reading is the part that
                the pattern cannot account for.
                Some of that gap has a cause, something happened on that road on
                that day that pushed congestion above its normal level.
                And some of it is just <term>natural variability</term>: small
                fluctuations that remain even after everything predictable has
                been accounted for.
                The chart shows all three types of variability at once: the
                repeating pattern (accounted for), the unexplained spike above
                it (unaccounted for), and the jagged noise around both lines
                (natural).
            </p>

            <p>
                <xref ref="fig-tomtom-live-congestion"/> displays a live
                congestion chart from TomTom.
                This makes all three types of variability visible in a single
                image.
                The dotted yellow line shows the usual congestion level for each
                time slot — the pattern that repeats week after week, the part
                that is accounted for.
                The orange line shows what actually happened on a particular
                Monday and Tuesday.
                The gap between the two lines is the part the usual pattern
                could not predict: something happened on those specific days
                that pushed congestion above its normal level.
                An accident, perhaps.
                A road closure.
                Something real, but unaccounted for.
                And the jagged noise around both lines — the small
                moment-to-moment fluctuations — is natural variability:
                irreducible randomness that would remain even if we knew
                everything measurable.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-tomtom-live-congestion">
                <caption>
                    Live versus usual congestion level in Nairobi across Monday
                    and Tuesday, with live speed shown below.
                    The orange line shows actual congestion; the dotted yellow
                    line shows the typical level for that time slot.
                    The gap between the two lines represents variation that the
                    usual pattern does not predict — unaccounted-for and natural
                    variability.
                    Source: TomTom Traffic Index, captured August 2026.
                </caption>

                <image source="images/ch9_tomtom_live_congestion.png" width="100%">
                    <description>
                        <p>
                            A line chart showing congestion level as a
                            percentage on the y-axis and time across Monday and
                            Tuesday on the x-axis.
                            An orange solid line shows live congestion and a
                            dotted yellow line shows the usual congestion level.
                            Both lines show peaks around 8am and 5pm on each
                            day.
                            On Monday afternoon, the orange live line spikes to
                            nearly 90 percent, substantially above the yellow
                            usual line of around 65 percent.
                            On Tuesday afternoon, the live line reaches over 100
                            percent and continues rising at the right edge of
                            the chart, labelled NOW, again running above the
                            usual level.
                            Below the congestion chart, a bar chart shows live
                            speed in km/h, with speeds dropping to around 10
                            km/h at peak congestion periods.
                        </p>
                    </description>
                </image>
            </figure>

            <p>
                Similar patterns have been documented in Kampala, Uganda, where
                analysis of close to a million boda-boda trips found significant
                variation in travel speed across different periods of the day.
                The accounted-for variation — the time-of-day pattern — was
                strong and consistent.
                But within any given time slot, individual trips still varied,
                reflecting the unaccounted-for and natural variability that no
                amount of time-of-day information could eliminate (Saddier,
                2025).
            </p>

            <p>
                <term>Sources:</term> TomTom Traffic Index 2025.
                Nairobi city profile.
                Available at:
                <url href="https://www.tomtom.com/traffic-index/city/nairobi/" visual="tomtom.com/traffic-index/city/nairobi"/>.
                Saddier, S.
                (2025).
                <q>Are motorcycle taxis competing with 
      collective public transport?</q>
                <em>Research in Transportation Economics</em>, 111, 101562.
                <url href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.retrec.2025.101562"/>.
            </p>
        </subsection>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="sec-ch9-variability-illustration">
        <title>Illustration of Variability</title>

        <p>
            Before measuring variability, it helps to see what the different
            types actually look like.
            We can build them up from scratch, starting with a dataset that has
            no variability at all.
        </p>

        <paragraphs>
            <title>No variability</title>

            <p>
                If <m>y</m> is always the same value regardless of anything
                else, there is nothing to explain and nothing to measure.
                Every point sits on a flat horizontal line.
            </p>
        </paragraphs>

        <program language="r">
            <code>
      x &lt;- 1:10
      y &lt;- rep(1, 10)
      df0 &lt;- data.frame(x, y)

      ggplot(df0, aes(x = x, y = y)) +
        geom_point() +
        geom_line() +
        labs(title = "No variability: y is always 1",
             y = "y", x = "x") +
        theme_bw()
            </code>
        </program>

        <figure xml:id="fig-ch9-no-variability">
            <caption>
                No variability.
                <m>y = 1</m> regardless of <m>x</m>.
                There is nothing to account for, and nothing to measure.
            </caption>

            <image source="images/ch9_no_variability.png" width="70%">
                <description>
                    <p>
                        A scatter plot with x on the horizontal axis and y on
                        the vertical axis.
                        All ten points sit at exactly y = 1, connected by a flat
                        horizontal line.
                        There is no spread in the y values at all.
                    </p>
                </description>
            </image>
        </figure>

        <paragraphs>
            <title>Accounted-for variability only</title>

            <p>
                Now let <m>y = x</m>.
                There is variability in <m>y</m> — the values change — but all
                of it is accounted for by <m>x</m>.
                As <m>x</m> increases, <m>y</m> increases by exactly the same
                amount.
                No randomness, no surprises.
            </p>
        </paragraphs>

        <program language="r">
            <code>
      x &lt;- 1:10
      y &lt;- x
      df1 &lt;- data.frame(x, y)

      ggplot(df1, aes(x = x, y = y)) +
        geom_point() +
        geom_line() +
        labs(title = "Accounted for variability only: y = x",
             y = "y", x = "x") +
        theme_bw()
            </code>
        </program>

        <figure xml:id="fig-ch9-explained-only">
            <caption>
                Accounted for variability only.
                <m>y = x</m>: all variability in <m>y</m> is accounted for by
                <m>x</m>.
            </caption>

            <image source="images/ch9_explained_only.png" width="70%">
                <description>
                    <p>
                        A scatter plot where all ten points fall exactly on a
                        straight diagonal line from the lower left to the upper
                        right.
                        There is no scatter around the line.
                    </p>
                </description>
            </image>
        </figure>

        <paragraphs>
            <title>Accounted for and natural variability</title>

            <p>
                Now add some random noise.
                The relationship between <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> is still there.
                <m>y</m> still tends to increase with <m>x</m>, but the points
                no longer fall exactly on the line.
                Some additional randomness is pushing them above or below where
                the line would predict.
                This is natural variability: noise that remains even after
                <m>x</m> is accounted for.
            </p>
        </paragraphs>

        <program language="r">
            <code>
      x &lt;- 1:10
      y &lt;- x + rnorm(10)
      df2 &lt;- data.frame(x, y)

      ggplot(df2, aes(x = x, y = y)) +
        geom_point() +
        geom_line(data = df1, aes(x = x, y = y),
                  linetype = "dashed", colour = "grey50") +
        labs(title = "Accounted for + natural variability: y = x + error",
             subtitle = "Dashed line shows y = x",
             y = "y", x = "x") +
        theme_bw()
            </code>
        </program>

        <figure xml:id="fig-ch9-explained-natural">
            <caption>
                Explained and natural variability.
                <m>y = x + \varepsilon</m>: points scatter around the line
                <m>y = x</m> (shown dashed) due to random noise.
                The trend is still visible but no longer perfect.
            </caption>

            <image source="images/ch9_explained_natural.png" width="70%">
                <description>
                    <p>
                        A scatter plot where the ten points are scattered around
                        a dashed diagonal line representing y = x.
                        Some points are above the line and some below, with no
                        obvious pattern to the deviations.
                        The overall upward trend is still visible.
                    </p>
                </description>
            </image>
        </figure>

        <paragraphs>
            <title>
                Accounted for, unaccounted-for, and natural variability
            </title>

            <p>
                Finally, introduce a second variable <m>z</m> that also
                influences <m>y</m>, but which we do not have in our dataset.
                In the code below, <m>z</m> takes the value 1 for the first five
                observations and 10 for the last five.
                We can see the effect it has: the points no longer scatter
                evenly around the line, but jump up noticeably in the second
                half.
                But if we did not know <m>z</m> existed, we would have no way to
                explain that jump.
                It would simply look like unusually large random variation.
            </p>
        </paragraphs>

        <program language="r">
            <code>
      x  &lt;- 1:10
      z  &lt;- c(rep(1, 5), rep(10, 5))  # hidden variable
      y  &lt;- x + z + rnorm(10)
      df3 &lt;- data.frame(x, y)

      ggplot(df3, aes(x = x, y = y)) +
        geom_point() +
        geom_smooth(method = "lm", se = FALSE,
                    colour = "#44575E", linewidth = 0.8) +
        labs(title = "Accounted for, unaccounted-for, and natural variability",
             subtitle = "The line fits poorly because z is not in the data",
             y = "y", x = "x") +
        theme_bw()
            </code>
        </program>

        <figure xml:id="fig-ch9-all-variability">
            <caption>
                Accounted for, unaccounted for, and natural variability.
                <m>y = x + z + \varepsilon</m>, but <m>z</m> is not in the data.
                The line of best fit through <m>x</m> alone fits poorly — the
                points in the second half jump above the line because of
                <m>z</m>, which we cannot see.
            </caption>

            <image source="images/ch9_all_variability.png" width="70%">
                <description>
                    <p>
                        A scatter plot where the first five points scatter
                        around the lower part of a fitted line and the last five
                        points scatter around a noticeably higher position.
                        The fitted line is a poor summary of the data because it
                        cannot account for the jump caused by the hidden
                        variable z.
                        The residuals are large and systematically patterned
                        rather than randomly scattered.
                    </p>
                </description>
            </image>
        </figure>

        <p>
            This last scenario is the most realistic.
            In the real world, we never have all the variables.
            There will always be something we have not measured that is
            influencing the outcome.
            The question is how much of the variability we can account for with
            what we do have — and how honest we are about what remains
            unaccounted for.
            The Nairobi traffic data is exactly this: time of day accounts for
            some of the variability in journey time, but unknown events
            (accidents, roadworks, broken-down matatus) account for more, and
            pure randomness accounts for the rest.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="sec-ch10-stack">
        <title>Variability STACK Question</title>

        <p>
            TODO: Insert STACK question on variability here.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="sec-accounting-variability-r">
        <title>Accounting for Variability in R</title>

        <insight xml:id="insight-boxplot-variability-types">
            # this goes into Ch10 I think
            <title>Two kinds of variability in one chart</title>

            <p>
                The chart in <xref ref="fig-ch9-africa-boxplot-years"/> shows
                two distinct kinds of variability at the same time, and it is
                worth separating them.
            </p>

            <p>
                The first is
                <term>variability across countries within a single 
  year</term>,
                shown by the height of each box.
                In any given year, some African countries have life expectancies
                well above the median and some well below.
                What drives this?
                Some of it could in principle be accounted for — if we had data
                on each country's wealth, access to healthcare, sanitation
                infrastructure, or disease burden, we could account for some of
                why one country sits above the box and another sits below it.
                The gapminder dataset gives us GDP per capita as a rough proxy
                for some of those things, but not all.
                Some drivers are harder to capture in data at all, and would
                hence be unaccounted for: governance quality, geography, the
                strength of public health systems, the legacy of particular
                conflicts.
                And some of the remaining variation between countries is simply
                natural: even two countries that look identical on every
                variable we can measure will not have exactly the same life
                expectancy.
            </p>

            <p>
                The second is
                <term>variability across years within Kenya</term>, illustrated
                by the rise and fall of the teal line.
                Kenya's life expectancy in 1952 was around 42 years; by 1987 it
                had risen to nearly 60; by 2002 it had fallen back to around 51,
                before recovering again.
                Again, some of it could in principle be accounted for.
                If we had data on improvements in medicine, sanitation, and
                nutrition over time, or on the spread of the HIV/AIDS epidemic,
                we could account for much of the rise and the subsequent dip.
                But we do not have those variables here.
                Some drivers are harder to capture in data at all: droughts,
                political instability, specific health policy decisions in
                particular years.
                These all affected Kenyan life expectancy but are rarely
                available as measured variables.
                And some is natural: even in a stable period, life expectancy
                fluctuates slightly from year to year for reasons no dataset
                would ever fully capture.
            </p>

            <p>
                You might notice that year is in our dataset, and we can see
                that life expectancy in Kenya changes depending on year.
                So does year account for the variability in Kenya's life
                expectancy over time?
                In a narrow sense, yes: if you know the year, you can predict
                roughly where Kenya's life expectancy will sit.
                But there is an important distinction between
                <term>accounting for</term> variability and
                <term>explaining</term> it.
                Year accounts for the pattern — it tells you when things
                changed.
                It does not explain why.
                The actual causes of the rise (improvements in medicine,
                sanitation, and nutrition) and the dip (the HIV/AIDS epidemic)
                are not in this dataset.
                Year is acting as a proxy for all of them combined.
                Accounting for variability means being able to predict or
                describe a pattern; explaining it means knowing what actually
                drove it.
                The two are not the same, and confusing them is one of the most
                common mistakes in data analysis.
            </p>
        </insight>

        <p>
            fig-ch9-least-squares-comparison -- The reduction in square area
            from left to right is the reduction in unaccounted-for variability.
        </p>

         <p>
            about the gif -- 
                The size of the squares (residuals) from this process are
                themselves a measure of variability: they represent everything
                in the outcome that the line cannot explain.
                A small total squared error means the line accounts for most of
                # discussing accounting for variability goes in Ch10.
                Just measuring it here, so we can call this back and discuss
                this in that context in ch10 the variability; a large total
                squared error means much of the variability is still unaccounted
                for.
            </p>
    </section>
    <exercises xml:id="exercises-ch10">
        <title>Exercises</title>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-variability-types">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    For each of the following situations, identify which type of
                    variability is being described (accounted-for,
                    unaccounted-for, or natural) and briefly explain your
                    reasoning.
                </p>

                <ol>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Matatu journey times in Nairobi are consistently
                            longer on Friday evenings than on Tuesday mornings.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Two students take the same boda-boda route on the
                            same Tuesday morning and arrive at slightly
                            different times, even though nothing unusual
                            happened on either journey.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Life expectancy in several southern African
                            countries dropped sharply in the 1990s due to the
                            HIV/AIDS epidemic, but this variable was not
                            included in the dataset being analysed.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            A farmer's crop yield varies from year to year.
                            The analyst has data on rainfall but not on soil
                            quality, which also affects yield.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </statement>
        </exercise>
    </exercises>
  <section xml:id="sec-accounting-africa">
    <title>Accounting for Variability in African Life Expectancy</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        In <xref ref="ch-variability"/>, we saw that the standard 
        deviation of life expectancy across African countries nearly 
        doubled between 1952 and 2007, and that specific events — 
        Rwanda in 1992, the HIV/AIDS epidemic in southern Africa from 
        the mid-1990s — drove sharp spikes in the variance. Those 
        observations described the variation. This section asks what 
        accounts for it.
      </p>
      <p>
        We will work with the same Africa data from the 
        <c>gapminder</c> package, and the same four highlighted 
        countries — Botswana, Kenya, Libya, and Swaziland. But instead 
        of asking how large the squares are, we will ask what makes 
        them large or small.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-accounting-year">
      <title>Year as a source of variability</title>

      <p>
        The simplest structure in this data is time. Life expectancy 
        changes year by year — generally upward, with some setbacks. 
        If we fit a line through Kenya's life expectancy over time, 
        as we did with the least squares visualisation in 
        <xref ref="ss-least-squares"/>, the line accounts for some 
        of the variability in the data. The squares around the line 
        — the residuals — are what the line cannot account for.
      </p>

      <p>
        But as we noted in <xref ref="ch-variability"/>, year is not 
        really an explanation. It is a label for when the observation 
        happened. The actual causes of the rise — improvements in 
        medicine, sanitation, and nutrition — and the cause of the 
        dip — the HIV/AIDS epidemic — are not directly in the dataset. 
        Year is acting as a proxy for all of them combined. It accounts 
        for the pattern, but does not explain it.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
          kenya &lt;- gapminder %&gt;%
            filter(country == "Kenya")

          # Fit a line through Kenya's life expectancy over time
          model_kenya &lt;- lm(lifeExp ~ year, data = kenya)

          # How much of the variation does year account for?
          summary(model_kenya)$r.squared
          #&gt; [1] 0.8878
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        The R-squared value of 0.89 means that year accounts for 
        roughly 89% of the variation in Kenya's life expectancy over 
        this period. The remaining 11% — the residuals around the 
        line — is variability that year alone cannot account for. Some 
        of that 11% is unaccounted-for variability with real causes 
        (droughts, policy decisions, specific health interventions that 
        happened in particular years). Some of it is natural variability 
        — small year-to-year fluctuations that no variable would fully 
        capture.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-accounting-country">
      <title>Country as a source of variability</title>

      <p>
        Now consider the variation across countries within a single 
        year. In 2007, the standard deviation of life expectancy across 
        all 52 African countries was 9.63 years. Where does that spread 
        come from?
      </p>

      <p>
        One variable available to us is GDP per capita. Wealthier 
        countries tend to have better healthcare systems, better 
        nutrition, and longer life expectancies. If we use GDP per 
        capita to account for some of the cross-country variation, how 
        much does it explain?
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
          africa_2007 &lt;- gapminder %&gt;%
            filter(continent == "Africa", year == 2007)

          model_gdp &lt;- lm(lifeExp ~ gdpPercap, data = africa_2007)
          summary(model_gdp)$r.squared
          #&gt; [1] 0.2137
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        GDP per capita accounts for only about 21% of the variation in 
        life expectancy across African countries in 2007. That is 
        meaningful — richer countries do tend to have longer life 
        expectancies — but most of the spread remains unaccounted for. 
        The remaining 79% reflects differences between countries that 
        GDP does not capture: governance, geography, disease burden, 
        the particular history of the HIV/AIDS epidemic in some 
        countries and not others. These are real causes of variation 
        — unaccounted-for variability — not random noise.
      </p>

      <p>
        We can see this directly by looking at the residuals. Countries 
        like Swaziland and Botswana sit far below what their GDP per 
        capita would predict — their life expectancies were pulled down 
        by HIV/AIDS in ways that GDP alone cannot account for. And Libya 
        sits well above what its GDP would predict — it benefited from 
        oil revenues being invested in public health in ways not 
        captured by a simple GDP figure.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
          library(dplyr)
          library(ggplot2)

          africa_2007 &lt;- africa_2007 %&gt;%
            mutate(
              fitted   = fitted(model_gdp),
              residual = residuals(model_gdp)
            )

          # Which countries have the largest residuals?
          africa_2007 %&gt;%
            select(country, lifeExp, fitted, residual) %&gt;%
            arrange(residual) %&gt;%
            head(5)
          #&gt; # A tibble: 5 x 4
          #&gt;   country   lifeExp fitted residual
          #&gt;   &lt;fct&gt;       &lt;dbl&gt;  &lt;dbl&gt;    &lt;dbl&gt;
          #&gt; 1 Swaziland    39.6   52.8    -13.2
          #&gt; 2 Mozambique   42.1   50.2    -8.1
          #&gt; 3 Zambia       42.4   51.1    -8.7
          #&gt; 4 Zimbabwe     43.5   51.4    -7.9
          #&gt; 5 Botswana     50.7   55.4    -4.7
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        The five countries with the largest negative residuals are all 
        among the most severely affected by HIV/AIDS. Their life 
        expectancies are 5 to 13 years lower than GDP per capita alone 
        would predict. That gap is unaccounted-for variability — real, 
        caused, and important — that a model using only GDP cannot see.
      </p>

    </subsection>

    <insight xml:id="insight-accounting-residuals">
      <title>Residuals are unaccounted-for variability</title>
      <p>
        In <xref ref="ss-least-squares"/>, we introduced residuals as 
        the distances between observed values and a fitted line. Now we 
        can name what they represent: they are the variability in the 
        outcome that the variables in the model cannot account for. 
        Large residuals do not necessarily mean the model is wrong — 
        they mean there is something real affecting the outcome that 
        the model has not measured. Swaziland's residual of −13.2 
        years is not noise. It is the HIV/AIDS epidemic, unmeasured.
      </p>
    </insight>

  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- SECTION 3: Partitioning variability — the ANOVA idea        -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <section xml:id="sec-anova-descriptive">
    <title>Partitioning Variability</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        So far we have thought about accounting for variability one 
        variable at a time. But there is a more systematic framework 
        for doing this: partitioning the total variability in an outcome 
        into the part that a set of variables accounts for, and the part 
        that remains. This framework is the foundation of what 
        statisticians call <term>analysis of variance</term>, or 
        <term>ANOVA</term> — though we will use it here purely as a way 
        of describing and measuring how variability is distributed, not 
        as a hypothesis test.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-anova-total">
      <title>Total variability, explained variability, residual variability</title>

      <p>
        Return to the variance squares from 
        <xref ref="fig-ch9-variance-squares"/>. The total area of all 
        those squares — added up — is proportional to the total variance 
        in the outcome. That is the total variability we are trying to 
        account for.
      </p>

      <p>
        When we fit a line — say, GDP per capita predicting life 
        expectancy — we are trying to reduce that total area. The line 
        moves through the data, and for each country, the square around 
        the line (the residual square) is smaller than the square around 
        the overall mean, if the line fits well. The reduction in total 
        square area is what the variable <em>accounts for</em>.
      </p>

      <p>
        We can put numbers on this:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
          # Total sum of squares (variability around the mean)
          SS_total &lt;- sum((africa_2007$lifeExp - 
                           mean(africa_2007$lifeExp))^2)

          # Residual sum of squares (variability around the line)
          SS_residual &lt;- sum(residuals(model_gdp)^2)

          # Explained sum of squares (what the model accounts for)
          SS_explained &lt;- SS_total - SS_residual

          # What fraction does GDP account for?
          SS_explained / SS_total
          #&gt; [1] 0.2137
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        The fraction of total variability accounted for by GDP per 
        capita is 0.214 — the same R-squared we saw earlier, now 
        expressed as a ratio of sums of squares. GDP accounts for 
        about 21% of the total squared variation in life expectancy 
        across African countries in 2007. The remaining 79% — the 
        residual sum of squares — is the variability that GDP cannot 
        account for.
      </p>

      <p>
        We can visualise this partitioning directly. The total squares 
        (from the mean) can be split into two parts for each country: 
        the part accounted for by the line (the distance from the mean 
        to the fitted value), and the part not accounted for (the 
        residual, from the fitted value to the observed point).
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
          ggplot(africa_2007, 
                 aes(x = reorder(country, lifeExp), y = lifeExp)) +
            # Residual segments (unaccounted-for)
            geom_segment(aes(xend = reorder(country, lifeExp),
                             y    = fitted,
                             yend = lifeExp),
                         colour = "#F37021", linewidth = 0.8) +
            # Explained segments (accounted-for by GDP)
            geom_segment(aes(xend = reorder(country, lifeExp),
                             y    = mean(lifeExp),
                             yend = fitted),
                         colour = "#44575E", linewidth = 0.8) +
            geom_hline(yintercept = mean(africa_2007$lifeExp),
                       linetype = "dashed", colour = "grey50") +
            geom_point(size = 2, colour = "#44575E") +
            coord_flip() +
            labs(
              title    = "Partitioning variability in African life expectancy, 2007",
              subtitle = "Dark teal: accounted for by GDP. Orange: residual (unaccounted for).",
              x        = NULL,
              y        = "Life expectancy (years)"
            ) +
            theme_bw() +
            theme(axis.text.y = element_text(size = 7))
        </code>
      </program>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch10-partitioned-variability">
        <caption>
          Partitioned variability in African life expectancy, 2007. 
          For each country, the dark teal segment shows the variability 
          accounted for by GDP per capita (the distance from the overall 
          mean to the fitted value). The orange segment shows the 
          residual — the variability that GDP cannot account for. 
          Countries where the orange segment dominates (Swaziland, 
          Mozambique, Zambia) are those where HIV/AIDS pulled life 
          expectancy far below what GDP would predict.
        </caption>
        <image source="images/ch10_partitioned_variability.png" width="100%">
          <description>
            <p>
              TODO: A horizontal bar chart with country names on the 
              y-axis and life expectancy on the x-axis. Each country 
              has two segments: a dark teal segment showing the 
              distance from the overall mean to the GDP-predicted value, 
              and an orange segment showing the residual from the 
              predicted value to the observed value. A dashed vertical 
              line marks the overall mean. Countries in southern Africa 
              (Swaziland, Mozambique, Zambia, Zimbabwe, Botswana) have 
              large orange segments below the teal, reflecting large 
              negative residuals from the HIV/AIDS epidemic.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-anova-table">
      <title>The ANOVA table as a summary of partitioned variability</title>

      <p>
        The partitioning we just computed — total sum of squares, 
        explained sum of squares, residual sum of squares — is usually 
        presented as an <term>ANOVA table</term>. In R:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
          anova(model_gdp)
          #&gt; Analysis of Variance Table
          #&gt;
          #&gt; Response: lifeExp
          #&gt;            Df  Sum Sq Mean Sq F value   Pr(&gt;F)
          #&gt; gdpPercap   1  966.1  966.1  13.56  0.0006 ***
          #&gt; Residuals  50 3561.5   71.2
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        Read the ANOVA table as a partition of the total variability:
      </p>

      <tabular halign="left">
        <col halign="left" width="30%"/>
        <col halign="left" width="35%"/>
        <col halign="left" width="35%"/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
          <cell>Row</cell>
          <cell>Sum of squares</cell>
          <cell>What it represents</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><c>gdpPercap</c></cell>
          <cell>966.1</cell>
          <cell>Variability accounted for by GDP per capita</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Residuals</cell>
          <cell>3561.5</cell>
          <cell>Variability not accounted for — the residuals</cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="minor">
          <cell>Total</cell>
          <cell>4527.6</cell>
          <cell>Total variability in life expectancy</cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>

      <p>
        GDP per capita accounts for 966.1 out of 4527.6 total units of 
        squared variability — about 21%. The remaining 3561.5 is 
        unaccounted for. That 3561.5 includes everything we saw in 
        <xref ref="ch-variability"/>: the HIV/AIDS epidemic in southern 
        Africa, the particular history of individual countries, and the 
        irreducible natural variability that would remain even if we 
        had measured everything measurable.
      </p>

      <p>
        We are not using the F-value or p-value in this chapter — those 
        are for hypothesis testing, which comes later. What matters here 
        is the partition itself: how much of the total variation does 
        each variable account for, and how much is left over.
      </p>

    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-anova-group">
      <title>Accounting for variability with a grouping variable</title>

      <p>
        GDP per capita is a continuous variable. But we can also account 
        for variability using a grouping variable — a factor. Continent 
        is an obvious candidate: do African countries cluster by 
        sub-region in ways that account for some of the variation in 
        life expectancy?
      </p>

      <p>
        We do not have sub-region in the gapminder data, but we can 
        illustrate the principle using the full gapminder dataset across 
        all continents, asking how much of the variation in life 
        expectancy is accounted for by which continent a country is in:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
          world_2007 &lt;- gapminder %&gt;% filter(year == 2007)

          model_continent &lt;- lm(lifeExp ~ continent, 
                                 data = world_2007)

          anova(model_continent)
          #&gt; Analysis of Variance Table
          #&gt;
          #&gt; Response: lifeExp
          #&gt;            Df  Sum Sq Mean Sq F value    Pr(&gt;F)
          #&gt; continent   4  8780.3  2195.1  83.29 &lt;2e-16 ***
          #&gt; Residuals  137  3612.7    26.4

          summary(model_continent)$r.squared
          #&gt; [1] 0.7086
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        Continent accounts for about 71% of the variation in life 
        expectancy across all countries in 2007. Knowing which 
        continent a country is on tells you a great deal about how 
        long its people can expect to live — far more than GDP per 
        capita alone told us for African countries. The remaining 29% 
        is the within-continent variation: the spread among countries 
        that share a continent but differ in life expectancy for other 
        reasons.
      </p>

      <p>
        This is exactly the kind of partition that matters for the 
        boxplot we saw in <xref ref="fig-ch9-africa-boxplot-years"/>. 
        The height of each box — the IQR — is within-group variability: 
        the spread among African countries in a given year, after 
        accounting for the fact that they are all in Africa. The 
        position of the box — where it sits on the y-axis — is 
        between-group variability: the difference between the African 
        average and the global average. ANOVA is the tool for 
        partitioning those two components formally.
      </p>

    </subsection>

  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- SECTION 4: Variability types revisited                       -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <section xml:id="sec-variability-types-revisited">
    <title>The Three Types, Revisited</title>

    <p>
      We can now return to the three types of variability from 
      <xref ref="sec-accounting-journeys"/> with the tools to be 
      precise about them.
    </p>

    <p>
      <term>Accounted-for variability</term> is the part of the total 
      sum of squares explained by the variables in the model — the dark 
      teal segments in <xref ref="fig-ch10-partitioned-variability"/>. 
      For the Africa data in 2007, GDP per capita accounts for 966.1 
      units of the total variability. Year accounts for most of the 
      trend in Kenya's life expectancy over time.
    </p>

    <p>
      <term>Unaccounted-for variability</term> is the part of the 
      residual sum of squares that has real causes — the HIV/AIDS 
      epidemic pulling Swaziland's residual to −13 years, the genocide 
      pulling Rwanda's residual to −29 years in 1992 — but those causes 
      are not variables we have in the dataset. This is the part of the 
      orange segments in the figure that we could, in principle, reduce 
      by measuring more things. It is not random. It just has not been 
      measured.
    </p>

    <p>
      <term>Natural variability</term> is the irreducible part of the 
      residual — the variation that would remain even if we had measured 
      everything measurable. It is the noise in the system: year-to-year 
      fluctuations in Kenya's life expectancy that cannot be attributed 
      to any identifiable cause. In practice, it is impossible to 
      separate natural variability from unaccounted-for variability 
      precisely — both live in the residuals. The distinction is 
      conceptual: some of what remains in the residuals has a cause 
      we have not found; some of it is genuinely random.
    </p>

    <insight xml:id="insight-anova-three-types">
      <title>ANOVA partitions the total — but cannot separate 
      unaccounted-for from natural variability</title>
      <p>
        The ANOVA table splits the total sum of squares into two parts: 
        what the model accounts for, and what it does not. But it 
        cannot tell you how much of the residual is unaccounted-for 
        variability with real unmeasured causes, and how much is 
        genuine random noise. That distinction requires domain knowledge 
        — understanding what else might be driving the outcome — not 
        just statistical tools. Rwanda in 1992 is in the residual of 
        any model that does not include a variable for genocide. The 
        statistics show the residual is large. Only history tells you 
        why.
      </p>
    </insight>

  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- EXERCISES                                                    -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <exercises xml:id="exercises-ch10">
    <title>Exercises</title>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-accounting-rsquared">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Using the <c>gapminder</c> dataset filtered to 2007:
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              Fit a linear model predicting life expectancy from GDP 
              per capita for African countries only. What is the 
              R-squared? How much variability does GDP account for?
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Now fit the same model for Asian countries only. Is 
              the R-squared higher or lower than for Africa? What 
              might explain the difference?
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Which five countries have the largest positive residuals 
              in the African model? Which five have the largest 
              negative residuals? What do the negative residuals have 
              in common?
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-accounting-continent">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Using the full <c>gapminder</c> dataset for 2007, fit a 
          model predicting life expectancy from continent.
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              Produce the ANOVA table. How much of the total 
              variability does continent account for?
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Now add GDP per capita to the model alongside continent. 
              Does adding GDP account for additional variability 
              beyond what continent already explains? How much?
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              In your own words, explain what the residual sum of 
              squares represents in each model.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-accounting-kenya">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Filter the gapminder data to Kenya only and fit a linear 
          model predicting life expectancy from year.
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              What is the R-squared? What does it tell you about how 
              much of Kenya's life expectancy variation is accounted 
              for by the passage of time?
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Plot the residuals over time. Is there a pattern, or 
              do they look random? What does any pattern suggest about 
              unaccounted-for variability?
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Which year has the largest negative residual? What 
              historical event explains it? Is this unaccounted-for 
              variability or natural variability — and how do you 
              know?
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-accounting-three-types">
      <statement>
        <p>
          For each of the following situations, say which type of 
          variability is being described — accounted-for, 
          unaccounted-for, or natural — and explain your reasoning.
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              A model predicting crop yield from rainfall accounts 
              for 65% of the variation in yield across farms. The 
              remaining 35% includes differences in soil quality that 
              were not measured.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              After accounting for soil quality and rainfall, a small 
              amount of variation in crop yield remains that cannot 
              be attributed to any identifiable factor.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              A model predicting Nairobi commute times using time of 
              day accounts for 40% of the variation. A specific 
              Friday's commutes were longer than usual because of an 
              accident on Ngong Road that was not recorded anywhere.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

  </exercises>

</chapter>